{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"freelance-client-onboarding-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Onboard a new freelance client","description":"Takes a freelancer from a client's yes to a running project in gated steps - paperwork and deposit, inputs and access, the kickoff call, then a two-week check-in that resets anything drifting.","category":"freelancing","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","build","operate","review"],"role":["consultant","designer","writer","software-engineer"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","document","notes"],"output":["checklist","message","docs","plan"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","tags":["client-onboarding","kickoff-call","deposits","project-setup","client-communication"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["write-statement-of-work","write-client-welcome-pack","negotiate-client-payment-terms","review-freelance-contract"],"personas":["freelance-business-advisor"]},"args":[{"name":"deal","description":"The client, what they said yes to (proposal or email), price, payment terms, timeline, and who you will work with on their side.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"how_you_work","description":"Your working hours, channels, revision rounds, tools for files and calls, and any terms of business you already have.","type":"text"}],"steps":[{"id":"paperwork","file":"steps/01-paperwork-and-deposit.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"onboarding/01-paperwork.md"},{"id":"inputs","file":"steps/02-inputs-and-access.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"onboarding/02-inputs.md"},{"id":"kickoff","file":"steps/03-kickoff-call.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve","artifact":"onboarding/03-kickoff.md"},{"id":"checkin","file":"steps/04-two-week-check-in.md","stage":"review","gate":"none","artifact":"onboarding/04-check-in.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Takes a freelancer from \"yes, let's do it\" to a project that runs smoothly. Most client problems start in the first two weeks: work begun before terms were signed or the deposit paid, inputs that never arrive, five people giving feedback, and habits (late-night messages, unpriced extras) that become the norm. This track settles the paperwork, collects what is needed, runs a kickoff that sets expectations, and checks in after two weeks to fix drift early. Each step writes one artifact and stops for approval.\n\n<deal>\n{{deal}}\n</deal>\n\n{{#how_you_work}}<how_you_work>\n{{how_you_work}}\n</how_you_work>{{/how_you_work}}\n\nRules for every step:\n- Use only facts the freelancer gave. Never invent prices, dates, clauses or names; mark gaps as [X].\n- No work starts before the agreed terms are in writing and any deposit is paid, unless the freelancer decides otherwise knowingly.\n- Client messages are short, friendly and specific, with one clear ask each.\n- Contract wording with significant money or rights at stake is for a lawyer to review; tax and registration questions go to an accountant. Say so once when relevant, not in every step.\n- Step 4 depends on what actually happened: ask for it before writing.","steps":[{"id":"paperwork","file":"steps/01-paperwork-and-deposit.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"onboarding/01-paperwork.md","text":"# Step 1: Paperwork and deposit\n\n1. List what must be in writing before work starts: scope and deliverables, price and payment schedule, timeline, revision limit, IP terms, and the contract or terms of business it sits under. Mark each as done, drafted or missing.\n2. Flag gaps or risks in the deal (no deposit, long payment terms, vague deliverables, no named decision-maker) with a suggested fix for each.\n3. Write the message that sends the agreement and the deposit invoice, with a target start date that depends on both being returned.\n4. Note supplier onboarding or purchase-order steps the client may need, if the client is a larger organisation.\n\nSections: Paperwork status, Gaps and fixes, Message, Client admin. Stop and wait for approval."},{"id":"inputs","file":"steps/02-inputs-and-access.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"onboarding/02-inputs.md","text":"# Step 2: Inputs and access\n\n1. List every input the project needs from the client (content, brand assets, data, examples, logins, decisions) with a due date and an owner on their side.\n2. Mark which inputs block which deliverables, and state how a late input moves the timeline.\n3. Write the request message: grouped list, one link or folder to upload to, due dates, and who to contact with questions.\n4. Access plan: which accounts the freelancer needs, the least access that works, how credentials will be shared safely (never in plain email or chat), and when access will be removed.\n\nSections: Inputs table, Dependencies, Request message, Access plan. Stop and wait for approval."},{"id":"kickoff","file":"steps/03-kickoff-call.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve","artifact":"onboarding/03-kickoff.md","text":"# Step 3: Kickoff call\n\n1. Write a 30 to 45 minute agenda: goals and success measures in the client's words, audience, deliverables walk-through, timeline and milestones, who gives feedback and how (one consolidated round per stage), communication hours and channels, and what happens when something changes.\n2. List the questions to ask that the proposal left open.\n3. Draft the follow-up recap email to send the same day: decisions, actions with owners and dates, next milestone, and anything still open.\n4. If the freelancer has a welcome pack, say where it fits; if not, list the points the recap must cover instead.\n\nSections: Agenda, Open questions, Recap email, Welcome pack note. Stop and wait for approval."},{"id":"checkin","file":"steps/04-two-week-check-in.md","stage":"review","gate":"none","artifact":"onboarding/04-check-in.md","text":"# Step 4: Two-week check-in\n\nAsk the freelancer what has happened since kickoff: inputs received or late, feedback pattern, extra requests, payment status, how the working relationship feels. Do not assume.\n\n1. Compare reality with the plan: inputs, timeline, scope, communication, payment. Mark each on track, drifting or off track.\n2. For each drifting item, propose a fix and the wording to raise it (a timeline reset, a scope-change note with a price, a reminder of feedback rules, a payment reminder).\n3. Write a short check-in message to the client that confirms progress, raises at most two issues, and sets the next milestone.\n4. Note what to change in the freelancer's onboarding for the next client.\n\nSections: Status, Fixes, Check-in message, Lessons for next time."}]}
